<h1>Run Dekopon on a Mac</h1>
<p>Run a Slack Agent in one review channel, with GitHub PR reads for an owner and colleague and a dedicated OpenRouter API key. Use native binaries and two macOS <code>launchd</code> services under separate accounts.</p>
<p><a class="button button-primary" href="{{ starter }}">Set up the Slack GitHub reviewer ↗</a></p>
<p>The <a href="{{ docs }}/run-on-mac.md">Mac runbook ↗</a> owns the installation and service templates. Match its configuration contracts to the release you install; the Homebrew tap can move ahead of this page.</p>
</header>
<nav class="guide-jumps" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#install">Binaries</a><a href="#slack-model">Slack and budget</a><a href="#github-profiles">Review access</a><a href="#pat">PAT and launchd</a><a href="#service">Service manager</a><a href="#verify">Before switching</a></nav>

<section id="install">
<h2>One verified archive supplies both daemons</h2>
<p>Install the Apple Silicon <a href="{{ release.url }}">release archive ↗</a> with the <a href="{{ docs }}/run-on-mac.md#prebuilt-archives">runbook's archive steps ↗</a>: they check its checksum and attestation, then point <code>/usr/local/lib/dekopon/current</code> at the <code>{{ release.version }}</code> directory. Every service template and recipe command runs the daemons from that path. Intel Macs have no archive and need a source build outside the release build matrix. The binaries declare macOS 11 as their minimum but are checked only on macOS 15; use Sequoia 15 or later.</p>
<p>Homebrew is the alternative: <code>brew install dekopon-agents/tap/dekopon</code>, then <code>brew pin dekopon</code>. Since Homebrew 6.0, installing the fully qualified name taps and trusts only this formula, and the pin keeps a routine <code>brew upgrade</code> off the binaries your services run. Those binaries live under Homebrew's prefix instead: substitute the literal <code>brew --prefix dekopon</code> output plus <code>/bin/</code> for every <code>/usr/local/lib/dekopon/current/</code> path, in the plists and gateway wrapper as well as the recipe's commands. launchd doesn't expand <code>$(…)</code>, and neither daemon account may write to that prefix. The runbook's <a href="{{ docs }}/run-on-mac.md#homebrew">Homebrew steps ↗</a> give the details.</p>
<p>Neither path installs a provider, configuration or service. The <a href="{{ starter }}">GitHub reviewer recipe ↗</a> supplies the provider pin, catalog and read-only policy; you still create the service accounts and map your own Slack members.</p>
<p>A <a href="{{ docs }}/development.md#macos-homebrew-compatibility">hosted macOS check ↗</a> installs the release from the tap and runs both daemons as same-user launchd jobs against synthetic Slack, GitHub and model services, using its own configuration instead of the recipe's files. You prove the rest on your own Mac: the recipe under separate service users, reboot and sleep recovery, and live Slack, GitHub and OpenRouter.</p>
</section>

<section id="slack-model">
<h2>Slack receives messages; OpenRouter bills inference</h2>
<p>Start with the <a href="{{ release.sourceUrl }}/examples/slack/manifest-agent.yaml">Slack Agent manifest ↗</a> from the same release as your binaries and set <code>experience: agent</code>. Socket Mode opens an outbound connection, so you don't need a public webhook, port forwarding or a local web server.</p>
<ol>
<li>Create and install the app, then collect its app-level <code>xapp-…</code> and bot <code>xoxb-…</code> tokens as the <a href="{{ release.sourceUrl }}/examples/slack/README.md#create-the-app-and-credentials">Slack setup guide ↗</a> shows.</li>
<li>Invite the app to one review channel. Record that workspace and channel plus both member IDs; use the recipe's exact channel route and broker mappings. Each person starts by mentioning the app. DMs and other channels are outside this route.</li>
<li>Configure <code>kind: openrouter</code> with a dedicated inference key. The gateway wrapper loads Slack and OpenRouter credentials from a private file; keep them out of the catalog, manifest and launchd plist.</li>
</ol>
<p>For private repositories, use a private Slack channel whose members may see the reviewed code and results. Invocation grants don't hide posted replies, and the selected model receives the reviewed content.</p>
<p>Slack's native Working/Stop UI depends on workspace plan and admin settings. The <a href="{{ release.sourceUrl }}/examples/slack/manifest.yaml">classic manifest ↗</a> with <code>experience: classic</code> is the fallback for workspaces without Agent features.</p>
<p>Set the key's dollar limit and reset period in OpenRouter, with a finite prepaid balance and automatic top-ups off; a periodic limit resets at midnight UTC (<a href="https://openrouter.ai/docs/api/api-reference/api-keys/create-a-new-api-key">OpenRouter's key limit fields ↗</a>). Avoid BYOK for this first setup: it adds a separate provider bill. Don't give Dekopon a management key. The key limit covers only requests billed through that key.</p>
<p>Dekopon's optional token meter is a second control, not a dollar cap: it resets on restart unless best-effort telemetry restore is configured, and journal summaries bypass it, so leave summaries off for the starter. Neither limit guarantees zero overshoot; watch actual OpenRouter usage.</p>
</section>

<section id="github-profiles">
<h2>Owner and colleague start with the same PR reads</h2>
<p>The <a href="{{ starter }}#broker-identities-and-read-only-grants">reviewer recipe ↗</a> maps two Slack users into the <code>pr-reviewers</code> group and maps the channel to <code>review-room</code>. These profiles are broker principal groups and Cedar rules; there is no <code>profiles</code> configuration key.</p>
<div class="guide-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="GitHub reviewer profiles">
<table>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Profile</th><th scope="col">Default access</th><th scope="col">Publishing</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Owner</th><td data-label="Default access">Prompt <code>pr-reviewer</code>; list, read and diff PRs, inspect status and read repository content.</td><td data-label="Publishing">Disabled. An optional owner-only <code>pr-publishers</code> grant can add a review comment.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Colleague</th><td data-label="Default access">The same read and analysis tools.</td><td data-label="Publishing">Disabled, including when the owner later opts in.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Review channel</th><td data-label="Default access">The broker requires both the sender and this channel to permit each request.</td><td data-label="Publishing">The channel must also permit the optional comment capability.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>The default grants no GitHub writes. The <a href="{{ starter }}#optional-owner-review-comments">separate opt-in ↗</a> permits a <code>COMMENT</code> review only; it doesn't grant approval, request-changes, push or merge. Once enabled, owner publishing has continuing permission without a per-write human approval gate. Use <a href="/guides/access/#profiles">the access guide</a> to understand the sender/channel intersection before extending it.</p>
<p>Both people use the broker's PAT: this is owner-delegated repository access, without a separate GitHub login check for each Slack user. Dekopon's trace can attribute the Slack sender; GitHub sees the PAT account. A fine-grained PAT limited to selected private repositories still has public-repository read access, and <code>allowedHosts: [api.github.com]</code> does not restrict repository names.</p>
</section>

<section id="pat">
<h2>The broker loads its PAT from a private file</h2>
<p>launchd doesn't source <code>.env</code> files. The gateway wrapper explicitly loads its Slack and OpenRouter environment variables; the broker has no dotenv parser or <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> substitution. Its plist starts <code>dekopon-brokerd</code> directly with the broker configuration path.</p>
<p>Create the broker's credential file exactly as the recipe's <a href="{{ starter }}#keep-the-github-pat-in-the-broker">PAT section ↗</a> shows: broker-owned, mode <code>0600</code>, holding the literal PAT. The broker's native HTTP code injects the PAT for the bound GitHub destination; the model and Wasm provider don't receive the value. Keep it out of the gateway environment, plist and repository. The runbook's <a href="{{ docs }}/run-on-mac.md#github-pat-under-launchd">PAT and launchd procedure ↗</a> covers ownership checks and rotation.</p>
</section>

<section id="service">
<h2>launchd can run both daemons before login</h2>
<p>For an always-on Mac, use two system LaunchDaemons with distinct, unprivileged users. That preserves the broker's credential boundary and lets the service run while nobody is logged in. An administrator must provision the accounts, owned directories and shared socket group. The formula defines no Homebrew service; the runbook <a href="{{ docs }}/run-on-mac.md#choose-the-process-manager">compares the other process managers ↗</a>.</p>
<p>In <a href="https://support.apple.com/guide/mac-help/set-sleep-and-wake-settings-mchle41a6ccd/mac">Energy settings ↗</a>, prevent automatic sleep while the display is off and turn on <strong>Start up automatically after a power failure</strong>; a sleeping Mac can't answer. With FileVault on, a restart after a power cut stops at the login window until someone enters a password, and launchd starts neither daemon before that. Don't turn off disk encryption to get unattended startup.</p>
<p>Logs can contain prompts and replies, and launchd doesn't rotate them: keep them private and bounded. Keep configuration, provider locks and persistent state in backed-up directories outside the Homebrew Cellar.</p>
</section>

<section id="verify">
<h2>Both reviewers need an allowed and a denied check</h2>
<p>The runbook's <a href="{{ docs }}/run-on-mac.md#check-before-installing-a-service">foreground checks ↗</a> run both configuration checks as their service users, start the broker, probe the socket as the broker user, then start the gateway. The probe checks the broker under its own UID; the real gateway and channel request check the separate-user path. A passing offline check doesn't prove credentials, network access or provider execution.</p>
<p>Follow the recipe's <a href="{{ starter }}#validate-and-accept">acceptance checks ↗</a>: both people can request a PR review in the configured channel, neither can publish under the default policy, and an unapproved member cannot start inference. Confirm that DMs and other channels don't route to this agent, and inspect actual OpenRouter usage.</p>
<p>Then exercise restart, logout and reboot recovery, including any FileVault unlock. Check logs and request another review after each. Upgrade both daemons together with the runbook's <a href="{{ docs }}/run-on-mac.md#sleep-logs-updates-and-recovery">update procedure ↗</a>, and keep the previous release archive with its configuration and provider set as the rollback pair. On Homebrew, unpin only inside that procedure (<code>brew unpin dekopon &amp;&amp; brew upgrade dekopon &amp;&amp; brew pin dekopon</code>): the upgrade deletes the previous version, migrates no configuration and leaves the running processes alone.</p>
<p>When replacing another assistant, test Dekopon with a separate Slack app first. Switch traffic only after these checks pass, and keep the prior configuration available for rollback. Dekopon doesn't import another assistant's memory, plugins or credentials automatically.</p>
<p><a href="{{ docs }}/run-on-mac.md">Read the latest in the Dekopon docs ↗</a> for release selection, configuration, launchd templates and recovery steps.</p>
</section>
